      "title": "The tree stays; its sampling mass changes",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume8-explain\" id=\"visual-V54\" data-v8=\"V54\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V54</span><h3>The tree stays; its sampling mass changes</h3></header><div class=\"v8-controls\" ><button type=\"button\" data-pause=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">a = 0</button><button type=\"button\" data-pause=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"true\">a = 1/4</button><button type=\"button\" data-pause=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">a = 1/2</button></div><label class=\"v8-range\">U <input type=\"range\" min=\"0\" max=\"8\" step=\"1\" value=\"4\" data-time aria-label=\"Illustrative uniform time\"><span data-time-label>1/2</span></label><picture data-v8-picture><source media=\"(max-width:700px)\" srcset=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V54-en-1-4-mobile.svg\"><img class=\"v8-scene\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V54-en-1-4-desktop.svg\" alt=\"The tree stays; its sampling mass changes\"></picture><div class=\"v8-print\"><section><h4>The tree stays; its sampling mass changes · 1/2</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V54.en.page1.svg\" alt=\"The tree stays; its sampling mass changes\"><p>The deterministic code rises linearly from 0 to 1, pauses for a, then returns linearly to 0, with a in {0,1/4,1/2}. Each sloping part lasts (1-a)/2. Its rooted quotient tree is always [0,1], but uniform time pushes forward to (1-a)Unif[0,1]+a delta_1. The thin line is geometry; band width is diffuse mass density; the top disk area is atomic mass. The slider chooses an illustrative U, not a new probability law. This is not a Brownian sample; atomlessness of Brownian CRT mass is a separate theorem.</p></section><section><h4>The tree stays; its sampling mass changes · 2/2</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V54.en.page2.svg\" alt=\"The tree stays; its sampling mass changes\"><p>The deterministic code rises linearly from 0 to 1, pauses for a, then returns linearly to 0, with a in {0,1/4,1/2}. Each sloping part lasts (1-a)/2. Its rooted quotient tree is always [0,1], but uniform time pushes forward to (1-a)Unif[0,1]+a delta_1. The thin line is geometry; band width is diffuse mass density; the top disk area is atomic mass. The slider chooses an illustrative U, not a new probability law. This is not a Brownian sample; atomlessness of Brownian CRT mass is a separate theorem.</p></section></div><output data-v8-result aria-live=\"polite\">Same metric tree; top mass 1/4, diffuse mass 3/4.</output><details><summary>Conditions, calculations and limits</summary><p>The deterministic code rises linearly from 0 to 1, pauses for a, then returns linearly to 0, with a in {0,1/4,1/2}. Each sloping part lasts (1-a)/2. Its rooted quotient tree is always [0,1], but uniform time pushes forward to (1-a)Unif[0,1]+a delta_1. The thin line is geometry; band width is diffuse mass density; the top disk area is atomic mass. The slider chooses an illustrative U, not a new probability law. This is not a Brownian sample; atomlessness of Brownian CRT mass is a separate theorem.</p><p><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0511515\">Le Gall (2005), §2.2, §2.5</a></p></details><figcaption>Exact finite models or deterministic constructions; diagrams do not replace proofs.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V54.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/figures/V54.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "The deterministic code rises linearly from 0 to 1, pauses for a, then returns linearly to 0, with a in {0,1/4,1/2}. Each sloping part lasts (1-a)/2. Its rooted quotient tree is always [0,1], but uniform time pushes forward to (1-a)Unif[0,1]+a delta_1. The thin line is geometry; band width is diffuse mass density; the top disk area is atomic mass. The slider chooses an illustrative U, not a new probability law. This is not a Brownian sample; atomlessness of Brownian CRT mass is a separate theorem.",
