      "title": "Separate alignments need not agree",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume8-explain\" id=\"visual-V55\" data-v8=\"V55\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V55</span><h3>Separate alignments need not agree</h3></header><div class=\"v8-controls\" ><button type=\"button\" data-witness=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Geometry</button><button type=\"button\" data-witness=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Mass</button><button type=\"button\" data-witness=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Joint compromise</button></div><picture data-v8-picture><source media=\"(max-width:700px)\" srcset=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V55-en-0-mobile.svg\"><img class=\"v8-scene\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V55-en-0-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Separate alignments need not agree\"></picture><div class=\"v8-print\"><section><h4>Separate alignments need not agree · 1/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V55.en.page1.svg\" alt=\"Separate alignments need not agree\"><p>X=Y=[-1,2], with root 0 and masses delta_1 and delta_-1. Three isometric placements of Y are identity, reflection, and reflection followed by translation by 1/2. Their current (Hausdorff,root,Prokhorov) errors are (0,0,1), (1,0,0), and (1/2,1/2,1/2). Two Dirac masses separated by 2 have Prokhorov distance 1 in the probability convention. Separately rGH=rGP=0, but rGHP=1/2: eccentricities of the mass points are 2 and 3, so any common witness with error r&lt;1 requires 3&lt;=2+2r. The third placement attains the bound. These are explicit witnesses for this interval example, not a general embedding algorithm.</p></section><section><h4>Separate alignments need not agree · 2/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V55.en.page2.svg\" alt=\"Separate alignments need not agree\"><p>X=Y=[-1,2], with root 0 and masses delta_1 and delta_-1. Three isometric placements of Y are identity, reflection, and reflection followed by translation by 1/2. Their current (Hausdorff,root,Prokhorov) errors are (0,0,1), (1,0,0), and (1/2,1/2,1/2). Two Dirac masses separated by 2 have Prokhorov distance 1 in the probability convention. Separately rGH=rGP=0, but rGHP=1/2: eccentricities of the mass points are 2 and 3, so any common witness with error r&lt;1 requires 3&lt;=2+2r. The third placement attains the bound. These are explicit witnesses for this interval example, not a general embedding algorithm.</p></section><section><h4>Separate alignments need not agree · 3/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V55.en.page3.svg\" alt=\"Separate alignments need not agree\"><p>X=Y=[-1,2], with root 0 and masses delta_1 and delta_-1. Three isometric placements of Y are identity, reflection, and reflection followed by translation by 1/2. Their current (Hausdorff,root,Prokhorov) errors are (0,0,1), (1,0,0), and (1/2,1/2,1/2). Two Dirac masses separated by 2 have Prokhorov distance 1 in the probability convention. Separately rGH=rGP=0, but rGHP=1/2: eccentricities of the mass points are 2 and 3, so any common witness with error r&lt;1 requires 3&lt;=2+2r. The third placement attains the bound. These are explicit witnesses for this interval example, not a general embedding algorithm.</p></section></div><output data-v8-result aria-live=\"polite\">Separately rGH=rGP=0; jointly rGHP=1/2. Diagram values describe the current placement.</output><details><summary>Conditions, calculations and limits</summary><p>X=Y=[-1,2], with root 0 and masses delta_1 and delta_-1. Three isometric placements of Y are identity, reflection, and reflection followed by translation by 1/2. Their current (Hausdorff,root,Prokhorov) errors are (0,0,1), (1,0,0), and (1/2,1/2,1/2). Two Dirac masses separated by 2 have Prokhorov distance 1 in the probability convention. Separately rGH=rGP=0, but rGHP=1/2: eccentricities of the mass points are 2 and 3, so any common witness with error r&lt;1 requires 3&lt;=2+2r. The third placement attains the bound. These are explicit witnesses for this interval example, not a general embedding algorithm.</p><p><a href=\"/chapters/55-metric-measure-limits.html\">Book §55.12–13; explicit interval calculation</a></p></details><figcaption>Exact finite models or deterministic constructions; diagrams do not replace proofs.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V55.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/figures/V55.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "X=Y=[-1,2], with root 0 and masses delta_1 and delta_-1. Three isometric placements of Y are identity, reflection, and reflection followed by translation by 1/2. Their current (Hausdorff,root,Prokhorov) errors are (0,0,1), (1,0,0), and (1/2,1/2,1/2). Two Dirac masses separated by 2 have Prokhorov distance 1 in the probability convention. Separately rGH=rGP=0, but rGHP=1/2: eccentricities of the mass points are 2 and 3, so any common witness with error r<1 requires 3<=2+2r. The third placement attains the bound. These are explicit witnesses for this interval example, not a general embedding algorithm.",
