      "title": "Length decides where a new branch attaches",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume8-explain\" id=\"visual-V57\" data-v8=\"V57\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V57</span><h3>Length decides where a new branch attaches</h3></header><div class=\"v8-controls\" ><button type=\"button\" data-graftu=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">U = 1/8</button><button type=\"button\" data-graftu=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"true\">U = 1/2</button><button type=\"button\" data-graftu=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">U = 7/8</button></div><div class=\"v8-controls\" ><button type=\"button\" data-exponential=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">E = 1</button><button type=\"button\" data-exponential=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"true\">E = 4</button><button type=\"button\" data-exponential=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">E = 8</button></div><div class=\"v8-controls\" ><button type=\"button\" data-after=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Before</button><button type=\"button\" data-after=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"true\">After</button></div><picture data-v8-picture><source media=\"(max-width:700px)\" srcset=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V57-en-1-1-1-mobile.svg\"><img class=\"v8-scene\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V57-en-1-1-1-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Length decides where a new branch attaches\"></picture><div class=\"v8-print\"><section><h4>Length decides where a new branch attaches · 1/2</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V57.en.page1.svg\" alt=\"Length decides where a new branch attaches\"><p>Condition on a two-leaf metric tree with edge lengths 1/2,1,1/2 and total s=2. A length-uniform attachment selects edges with probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4, not 1/3 when those lengths are known. Independent illustrative inputs U,E give arclength sU, new total L=sqrt(s²+E/2), and branch length L-s. With E exponential of rate 1 this is the standard-e clock 4r dr; buttons merely specify inputs. Grafting preserves old distances. Empirical birth-leaf mass is not normalized skeleton length; finite grafting does not prove infinite compactness or existence of mass. Aldous uses r dr and the 2e scale, so lengths are halved here.</p></section><section><h4>Length decides where a new branch attaches · 2/2</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V57.en.page2.svg\" alt=\"Length decides where a new branch attaches\"><p>Condition on a two-leaf metric tree with edge lengths 1/2,1,1/2 and total s=2. A length-uniform attachment selects edges with probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4, not 1/3 when those lengths are known. Independent illustrative inputs U,E give arclength sU, new total L=sqrt(s²+E/2), and branch length L-s. With E exponential of rate 1 this is the standard-e clock 4r dr; buttons merely specify inputs. Grafting preserves old distances. Empirical birth-leaf mass is not normalized skeleton length; finite grafting does not prove infinite compactness or existence of mass. Aldous uses r dr and the 2e scale, so lengths are halved here.</p></section></div><output data-v8-result aria-live=\"polite\">Length weights: 1/4,1/2,1/4. Clock 4r dr for standard e.</output><details><summary>Conditions, calculations and limits</summary><p>Condition on a two-leaf metric tree with edge lengths 1/2,1,1/2 and total s=2. A length-uniform attachment selects edges with probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4, not 1/3 when those lengths are known. Independent illustrative inputs U,E give arclength sU, new total L=sqrt(s²+E/2), and branch length L-s. With E exponential of rate 1 this is the standard-e clock 4r dr; buttons merely specify inputs. Grafting preserves old distances. Empirical birth-leaf mass is not normalized skeleton length; finite grafting does not prove infinite compactness or existence of mass. Aldous uses r dr and the 2e scale, so lengths are halved here.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.stat.berkeley.edu/~aldous/Papers/CRT3.pdf\">Aldous (1993), §4.3, pp.275–276</a></p></details><figcaption>Exact finite models or deterministic constructions; diagrams do not replace proofs.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V57.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/figures/V57.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Condition on a two-leaf metric tree with edge lengths 1/2,1,1/2 and total s=2. A length-uniform attachment selects edges with probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4, not 1/3 when those lengths are known. Independent illustrative inputs U,E give arclength sU, new total L=sqrt(s²+E/2), and branch length L-s. With E exponential of rate 1 this is the standard-e clock 4r dr; buttons merely specify inputs. Grafting preserves old distances. Empirical birth-leaf mass is not normalized skeleton length; finite grafting does not prove infinite compactness or existence of mass. Aldous uses r dr and the 2e scale, so lengths are halved here.",
