      "title": "Compare two public k-ary models",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume8-explain\" id=\"visual-V58\" data-v8=\"V58\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V58</span><h3 data-v8-heading>k=3: event B has different probabilities</h3></header><div class=\"v8-controls\" ><button type=\"button\" data-arity=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">k = 2</button><button type=\"button\" data-arity=\"3\" aria-pressed=\"true\">k = 3</button><button type=\"button\" data-arity=\"4\" aria-pressed=\"false\">k = 4</button></div><div class=\"v8-controls\" ><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Edge 0</button><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Edge 1</button><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Edge 2</button><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"3\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Edge 3</button><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"4\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Edge 4</button><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"5\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Edge 5</button><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"6\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Edge 6</button><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"7\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Edge 7</button><button type=\"button\" data-growedge=\"8\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Edge 8</button></div><picture data-v8-picture><source media=\"(max-width:700px)\" srcset=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V58-en-3-2-mobile.svg\"><img class=\"v8-scene\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V58-en-3-2-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Compare two public k-ary models\"></picture><details><summary>Public models and continuum-limit boundaries</summary><p>Fixed-k conditioned finite-variance GW uses vertex mass and square-root scaling. Haas–Stephenson growth uses leaf mass and I^(-1/k) scaling; the book has not fully reproved its general whole-tree tightness theorem. Finite counts do not prove that theorem or identify any unpublished CRT. Stable Lévy trees and ICRT are other models, and no classification or mass formula for them is added here.</p></details><div class=\"v8-print\"><section><h4>Compare two public k-ary models · 1/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V58.en.page1.svg\" alt=\"Compare two public k-ary models\"><p>Public models only: fixed k in {2,3,4}, critical GW offspring P(0)=1-1/k, P(k)=1/k, conditioned on I=3 internal vertices (N=3k+1), uniform over plane trees; versus the Haas–Stephenson (2015) uniform-edge growth rule, inserting a vertex and k-1 branches with unit combinatorial edges. Event B is that the internal root has two internal children. For k=2 both probabilities are 1/5; equality of this one event alone does not identify the complete laws. For k=3,4 the event probabilities differ. GW has C(k,2) such plane shapes out of k(3k-1)/2; at growth step 3 there are 2k+1 equiprobable edges, of which k-1 give that event. Only this unlabeled root event is compared. Birth labels, vertex mass and leaf mass are distinct. Growth has a planted root edge; GW does not. Counts are independently enumerated from public rules, with no unpublished formula or density. Finite counts are not a continuum-limit proof.</p></section><section><h4>Compare two public k-ary models · 2/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V58.en.page2.svg\" alt=\"Compare two public k-ary models\"><p>Public models only: fixed k in {2,3,4}, critical GW offspring P(0)=1-1/k, P(k)=1/k, conditioned on I=3 internal vertices (N=3k+1), uniform over plane trees; versus the Haas–Stephenson (2015) uniform-edge growth rule, inserting a vertex and k-1 branches with unit combinatorial edges. Event B is that the internal root has two internal children. For k=2 both probabilities are 1/5; equality of this one event alone does not identify the complete laws. For k=3,4 the event probabilities differ. GW has C(k,2) such plane shapes out of k(3k-1)/2; at growth step 3 there are 2k+1 equiprobable edges, of which k-1 give that event. Only this unlabeled root event is compared. Birth labels, vertex mass and leaf mass are distinct. Growth has a planted root edge; GW does not. Counts are independently enumerated from public rules, with no unpublished formula or density. Finite counts are not a continuum-limit proof.</p></section><section><h4>Compare two public k-ary models · 3/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V58.en.page3.svg\" alt=\"Compare two public k-ary models\"><p>Public models only: fixed k in {2,3,4}, critical GW offspring P(0)=1-1/k, P(k)=1/k, conditioned on I=3 internal vertices (N=3k+1), uniform over plane trees; versus the Haas–Stephenson (2015) uniform-edge growth rule, inserting a vertex and k-1 branches with unit combinatorial edges. Event B is that the internal root has two internal children. For k=2 both probabilities are 1/5; equality of this one event alone does not identify the complete laws. For k=3,4 the event probabilities differ. GW has C(k,2) such plane shapes out of k(3k-1)/2; at growth step 3 there are 2k+1 equiprobable edges, of which k-1 give that event. Only this unlabeled root event is compared. Birth labels, vertex mass and leaf mass are distinct. Growth has a planted root edge; GW does not. Counts are independently enumerated from public rules, with no unpublished formula or density. Finite counts are not a continuum-limit proof.</p></section></div><output data-v8-result aria-live=\"polite\">k=3: GW gives 3/12; public growth gives 2/7. Arity does not determine the law.</output><details><summary>Conditions, calculations and limits</summary><p>Public models only: fixed k in {2,3,4}, critical GW offspring P(0)=1-1/k, P(k)=1/k, conditioned on I=3 internal vertices (N=3k+1), uniform over plane trees; versus the Haas–Stephenson (2015) uniform-edge growth rule, inserting a vertex and k-1 branches with unit combinatorial edges. Event B is that the internal root has two internal children. For k=2 both probabilities are 1/5; equality of this one event alone does not identify the complete laws. For k=3,4 the event probabilities differ. GW has C(k,2) such plane shapes out of k(3k-1)/2; at growth step 3 there are 2k+1 equiprobable edges, of which k-1 give that event. Only this unlabeled root event is compared. Birth labels, vertex mass and leaf mass are distinct. Growth has a planted root edge; GW does not. Counts are independently enumerated from public rules, with no unpublished formula or density. Finite counts are not a continuum-limit proof.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.math.univ-paris13.fr/~haas/AIHPB2015514.pdf\">Haas–Stephenson (2015), model p.1314; Theorem 1.1</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0511515\">Le Gall (2005), §1, conditioned plane trees</a></p></details><figcaption>Exact finite models or deterministic constructions; diagrams do not replace proofs.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume8-v1/V58.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/figures/V58.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Public models only: fixed k in {2,3,4}, critical GW offspring P(0)=1-1/k, P(k)=1/k, conditioned on I=3 internal vertices (N=3k+1), uniform over plane trees; versus the Haas–Stephenson (2015) uniform-edge growth rule, inserting a vertex and k-1 branches with unit combinatorial edges. Event B is that the internal root has two internal children. For k=2 both probabilities are 1/5; equality of this one event alone does not identify the complete laws. For k=3,4 the event probabilities differ. GW has C(k,2) such plane shapes out of k(3k-1)/2; at growth step 3 there are 2k+1 equiprobable edges, of which k-1 give that event. Only this unlabeled root event is compared. Birth labels, vertex mass and leaf mass are distinct. Growth has a planted root edge; GW does not. Counts are independently enumerated from public rules, with no unpublished formula or density. Finite counts are not a continuum-limit proof.",
